introduction:
  bigQuestion: "How do programs make decisions and repeat tasks efficienty?"
  overview: "This unit moves from data storage to the mechanics of logic. You will explore how programs decide what to do next (<strong>Selection</strong>), how they repeat tasks (<strong>Iteration</strong>), and how we organise code into reusable modules. You will also learn to measure the <strong>efficiency</strong> of your solutions using <strong>Big O Notation</strong>."
  journey: "You'll start with fundamental logic control (if/else, loops). Then, you'll apply this to master standard algorithms for <strong>sorting</strong> and <strong>searching</strong> data, analyzing which methods are faster. Finally, you'll learn to make your data persist by reading and writing to <strong>files</strong>."

concepts:
  - title: "Logic & Control Flow"
    icon: "fa-code-branch"
    content: |
      <p>Programs are not just linear lists of instructions. We control the flow using:</p>
      <ul>
          <li><strong>Selection:</strong> Making decisions (e.g., <code>if password == "123": enter()</code>).</li>
          <li><strong>Iteration:</strong> Repeating tasks (e.g., <code>while user_is_active: keep_running()</code>).</li>
          <li><strong>Sequencing:</strong> Ensuring instructions happen in the correct order.</li>
      </ul>

  - title: "Standard Algorithms"
    icon: "fa-arrow-down-a-z"
    content: |
      <p>Computer Science has standard "recipes" for common problems. You will master:</p>
      <ul>
          <li><strong>Searching:</strong> Finding an item. <em>Linear Search</em> (check one by one) vs <em>Binary Search</em> (divide and conquer).</li>
          <li><strong>Sorting:</strong> Organizing data. <em>Bubble Sort</em> (swapping neighbors) vs <em>Selection Sort</em> (finding the minimum).</li>
      </ul>

  - title: "Efficiency (Big O)"
    icon: "fa-gauge-high"
    content: |
      <p>Not all correct solutions are good solutions. We measure efficiency using <strong>Big O Notation</strong> to predict how an algorithm performs as data grows.</p>
      <ul>
          <li><strong>O(1):</strong> Constant time (instant).</li>
          <li><strong>O(n):</strong> Linear time (more data = more time).</li>
          <li><strong>O(n²):</strong> Quadratic time (slow! e.g., nested loops).</li>
      </ul>

  - title: "File Processing"
    icon: "fa-file-import"
    content: |
      <p>Variables die when the program ends. To save data, we use <strong>Persistent Storage</strong>.</p>
      <ul>
          <li><strong>Read:</strong> Loading data from a file into memory.</li>
          <li><strong>Write:</strong> Overwriting a file with new data.</li>
          <li><strong>Append:</strong> Adding new data to the end of an existing file.</li>
      </ul>

terminology:
  - term: "Selection"
    definition: "A programming construct where a section of code is run only if a condition is met (If/Else)."
  - term: "Iteration"
    definition: "Repeating a block of code a specific number of times (For) or until a condition changes (While)."
  - term: "Big O Notation"
    definition: "A formal notation used to describe the performance or complexity of an algorithm."
  - term: "Linear Search"
    definition: "Checking every element in a list one by one until the target is found."
  - term: "Binary Search"
    definition: "An efficient search that divides a <strong>sorted</strong> list in half repeatedly."
  - term: "Bubble Sort"
    definition: "A simple sort that repeatedly steps through the list, swaps adjacent elements if they are in the wrong order."
  - term: "Selection Sort"
    definition: "A sort that repeatedly finds the minimum element and moves it to the beginning."
  - term: "Persistent Storage"
    definition: "Data storage that retains data after the power to the computer is turned off (e.g., Files)."

misconceptions:
  - mistake: "Binary Search on Unsorted Lists"
    correction: "Attempting to run Binary Search on a jumbled list. It won't work! The data MUST be sorted first."
  - mistake: "Bubble Sort Efficiency"
    correction: "Believing Bubble Sort is 'good' because it's easy to code. It is actually very slow (O(n²)) for large datasets."
  - mistake: "File Modes"
    correction: "Confusing 'w' (write - which deletes existing content) with 'a' (append - which adds to it)."

